<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hosokawa Tadaoki</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1563">1563</a>–<a href="/years/1646">1646</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hosokawa Tadaoki was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> master who fought for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Sekigahara</a> in 1600. His wife, the Christian Hosokawa Gracia, died rather than be taken hostage by Ieyasu's enemies. He was rewarded with Buzen province, where he built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokura">Kokura</a> castle, and was a leading disciple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sen-no-rikyu">Sen no Rikyū</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hosokawa-tadaoki.jpg" alt="Hosokawa Tadaoki" width="320" height="304" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 不明。 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hosokawa_Tadaoki.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
